<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Frunze</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Frunze</a> was a Bolshevik revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> commander. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> he defeated Kolchak's forces in the Urals, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1920 and drove Wrangel's army from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> later that year. He succeeded Trotsky as People's Commissar for Military and Naval Affairs in 1925 and died after surgery that year; the city of Bishkek bore his name from 1926 to 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze.jpg" alt="Mikhail Frunze" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michai%C5%82_Frunze_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mikhail Vasilyevich Frunze was born on 2 February 1885 in Pishpek, now Bishkek, then a small Russian garrison town in Russian Turkestan. His father was a Bessarabian Romanian paramedic and his mother was Russian. He began higher studies at Verniy, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almaty">Almaty</a>, and in 1904 entered the Saint Petersburg Polytechnical University. In the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party he sided at eighteen with Lenin's Bolsheviks. In the 1905 Revolution he led striking textile workers in Shuya and Ivanovo. Arrested in 1907, he was sentenced to death, spent months on death row, and had the sentence commuted to life at hard labour. After ten years in Siberian prisons he escaped to Chita and edited a Bolshevik weekly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Frunze</a> headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> civilian militia and was elected president of the Byelorussian Soviet, then led an armed force of workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In March 1919 he took command of the Southern Army Group of the Eastern Front. After his troops defeated Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Alexander Kolchak</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omsk">Omsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> gave him overall command of the Eastern Front on 19 July 1919. He drove Basmachi insurgents and White troops from Turkestan and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> in February 1920 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in September 1920. In November 1920 his army took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and pushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-wrangel">Pyotr Wrangel</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and he also led the destruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor-makhno">Nestor Makhno</a>'s anarchist movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>.</p><p>In December 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Frunze</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> as an ambassador of the Ukrainian SSR and established Turkish-Soviet relations. His diary of the visit records massacres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> that he saw in the Pontus region. He was elected to the Central Committee in 1921, became a candidate member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> on 2 June 1924 and in January 1925 was named chairman of the Revolutionary Military Council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Frunze</a> had a chronic ulcer and favoured conservative treatment, but Stalin and Anastas Mikoyan urged an operation. He died during surgery on 31 October 1925, and a number of historians attribute his death to chloroform poisoning. Rumours have held that Stalin arranged it; the claim rests on the accounts of authors such as Roman Brackman and Vadim Rogovin. He was buried in the Kremlin Wall Necropolis. Pishpek was renamed Frunze in 1926 and took back its name Bishkek in 1991. The Frunze Military Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and many streets and Metro stations were named after him, and some remember him for his military doctrine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mikhail Frunze” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In September 1920 the Red Army under <b>Mikhail Frunze</b>, supporting a revolt by the Young Bukharans and Bukharan communists, captured Bukhara and drove out the last emir.</blockquote><a href="/state/bukharan-peoples-soviet-republic">Bukharan People's Soviet Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.87,74.57&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Bishkek on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6352 641 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mikhail Frunze"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6802 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6802 911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.87,74.57&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
